

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Design, section 1 of 3 — [1marchitecture and data flow.[0m

  Three new files, all ES modules, no build step:

  - [38;5;153msrc/validation/rules.js[39m — the rule factories. [38;5;153mrequired()[39m, [38;5;153mminLength(n)[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength(n)[39m, [38;5;153memail()[39m, [38;5;153mmatches(otherField)[39m,
    [38;5;153mpattern(re, message)[39m, [38;5;153mcustom(fn, message)[39m. Each returns [38;5;153m{ name, message, test(value, allValues) }[39m. Pure, no DOM, no
    imports.
  - [38;5;153msrc/validation/validate.js[39m — the engine. [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m walks the schema, runs each field's rules in
    order, stops at that field's first failure but keeps going across other fields. Returns [38;5;153m{ valid: boolean, errors: {
[39m    [38;5;153mfieldName: message } }[39m — [38;5;153merrors[39m is empty when valid. Also pure and DOM-free.
  - [38;5;153msrc/validation/bind.js[39m — the DOM layer, and the only file that touches [38;5;153mdocument[39m. [38;5;153mbindForm(formElement, schema, [39m
    [38;5;153monValid)[39m attaches a submit handler, reads values via [38;5;153mnew FormData(form)[39m, calls [38;5;153mvalidate[39m, renders errors on failure,
    and calls [38;5;153monValid(values)[39m on success.

  Data flow on submit: [38;5;153mbindForm[39m's handler calls [38;5;153mpreventDefault[39m, builds a values object from [38;5;153mFormData[39m, hands it to
  [38;5;153mvalidate[39m along with the schema, and branches — errors get rendered per field and nothing else happens; a clean result
  calls [38;5;153monValid(values)[39m, which for login is the existing [38;5;153mlogin()[39m call.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes the login form's wiring only: import the pieces, declare the login schema, call [38;5;153mbindForm[39m. The current
  [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m function goes away — its behavior is the schema [38;5;153m{ username: [required()], password: [required()] }[39m.

  Two notes on the split. The engine never imports the rules — it only calls [38;5;153mtest[39m, so a schema can mix built-in
  factories and inline [38;5;153mcustom[39m rules without the engine knowing the difference. And [38;5;153mmatches(otherField)[39m is why [38;5;153mtest[39m takes
  [38;5;153mallValues[39m rather than just [38;5;153mvalue[39m; that signature is the one thing here that's awkward to change later, since every
  rule would need rewriting.

  [1mScope I'm assuming, correct me if wrong:[0m this task builds the mechanism and converts the existing login form, and adds
  the signup form (markup plus schema) as the second consumer that proves the reuse. Without a real second form,
  "reusable" is untested.

  Does section 1 look right?

